+# Bug Assessment: Category deletion removes products and breaks references
+
+- **Slug**: ticket
+- **Created**: 2026-11-18T00:00:00+00:00
+- **Source**: pasted text
+- **Verdict**: valid
+- **Severity**: critical
+
+## Report (verbatim or summarized)
+
+A deleted category currently causes products that were only assigned to it to disappear, breaking carts and orders that reference those products. Product category assignments, descendants, not-found behavior, and deletion of an unknown category must all follow the ticket requirements.
+
+## Symptom
+
+DELETE `/api/v1/private/category/{id}` deletes products while deleting a category, so product, cart, and order data is lost or becomes unusable. A deleted category can also produce a server error when fetched instead of HTTP 404. The expected behavior is to remove only the category subtree and detach those categories from products while preserving products and their commercial data.
+
+## Reproduction
+
+1. Create a category with a child category and assign products to the parent/child, including a product with another category and a product referenced by a cart or order.
+2. DELETE `/api/v1/private/category/{id}`.
+3. Verify the products, cart/order operations, category listings, product category output, and GET/DELETE behavior for the deleted id.
+
+## Suspected Code Paths
+
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/services/catalog/category/CategoryServiceImpl.java:231-284` — category deletion finds products in the subtree, removes category links, but calls `productService.delete` when the product has no remaining category; this explicitly deletes products and their dependent data.
+- `sm-core-model/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/model/catalog/category/Category.java:61-62` — descendant categories use `cascade = CascadeType.REMOVE`, matching subtree deletion but making deletion order and association cleanup important.
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/facade/category/CategoryFacadeImpl.java:360-365` — public deletion entry point resolves the category and delegates to the service.
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/api/v1/category/CategoryApi.java:65-76,222-235` — category GET and DELETE endpoints expose the not-found and deletion behavior.
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/api/v1/product/ProductApi.java:175-240` — product filtering accepts `category`; a deleted category should therefore naturally return no products.
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/services/catalog/product/ProductServiceImpl.java:215-243` — product deletion nulls categories and deletes product-related data, explaining cart/order and product loss when invoked from category deletion.
+
+## Root Cause Hypothesis
+
+High confidence: `CategoryServiceImpl.delete` treats a product with no categories left after detachment as disposable and calls the product deletion workflow. That workflow removes the product and associated data, violating the required category-only deletion semantics. The category service also does not clearly expose a not-found result from its void delete operation, while read paths may dereference a missing category, so the fix should make deletion explicitly fail with `ResourceNotFoundException` at the facade boundary and ensure category associations are removed without deleting products.
+
+## Proposed Remediation
+
+**Preferred**: Change category deletion to gather the category subtree, detach every affected product from all categories in that subtree, and persist the product even when no categories remain. Never call `productService.delete` from category deletion. Delete descendants and the root category in a safe order while preserving product, inventory, price, cart, and order rows. Make the private DELETE path resolve the category for the current store and throw the existing not-found exception when absent; ensure GET and product filtering return 404/empty results through their existing null handling.
+
+Add focused service/API integration coverage for products assigned only to the deleted subtree, products with another category, descendants, deleted-category GET/list/product filtering, unknown DELETE, and cart/order preservation.
+
+**Files likely to change**:
+- `sm-core/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/core/business/services/catalog/category/CategoryServiceImpl.java`
+- `sm-shop/src/main/java/com/salesmanager/shop/store/facade/category/CategoryFacadeImpl.java`
+- `sm-shop/src/test/java/com/salesmanager/test/shop/integration/category/CategoryManagementAPIIntegrationTest.java`
+- Relevant core category tests if service-level coverage is more appropriate.
+
+**Tests to add or update**:
+- Delete a category subtree and assert all products remain, with subtree category links removed and other links retained.
+- Assert category GET is 404, category listing excludes the deleted subtree, and product filtering by the deleted id is empty.
+- Assert deleting an unknown category is 404.
+- Assert an existing cart and order containing an affected product remain readable and usable.
+
+## Risks & Considerations
+
+- JPA many-to-many join-table updates must be flushed before category rows are removed to avoid foreign-key or stale-session errors.
+- Descendant deletion must be ordered safely and scoped to the merchant store; cross-store category ids must not be deleted.
+- Existing product delete behavior must remain unchanged for explicit product deletion.
+- Full cart/order end-to-end coverage may require the integration test fixture and database configuration.
+
+## Open Questions
+
+- [NEEDS CLARIFICATION: The supplied ticket does not identify the exact integration fixture/API setup for creating carts and orders; use existing shop integration helpers.]
